WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.230
Has the Penguin always been the proprietor
of a nightclub in Gotham?

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:09.030
And what does the Iceberg Lounge mean for
the character and his criminal operations?

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.820
Keep watching to find out.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.469
Oswald Cobblepot, known to many as The Penguin,
has proved to be quite the monocle-wearing,

00:00:15.469 --> 00:00:21.220
umbrella-wielding thorn in the Dark Knight's
side since his debut in December 1941's "Detective

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.800
Comics" #58.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.140
The character has definitely evolved over
his 80-plus years of existence, going from

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.980
bird-themed crook to politician to businessman
and back again.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:36.680
He's been a little silly, like his portrayal
by Burgess Meredith in the 1966 "Batman" TV

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:41.050
show, and monstrous, like when Danny DeVito
played him in "Batman Returns" in 1992.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.320
"You gotta admit!

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.770
I played this stinkin' city like a harp from
hell!"

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:52.190
Some of the more drastic character alterations
tend to disappear from version to version,

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:56.020
but every now and again, some new twist to
the Penguin will stick around.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.200
For instance, something that has relatively
recently become a key element of the Penguin

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.309
is his position as the operator of the Iceberg
Lounge.

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:08.350
The seedy nightclub been around for just under
30 years, debuting on the pages of "Detective

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.470
Comics" #683, in March of 1995.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:16.180
In his early days of terrorizing Gotham City,
the Penguin stood as a figurehead of the criminal

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.180
underworld.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:22.470
Lacking superhuman abilities or the more homicidal
tendencies of his supervillain peers, Cobblepot

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.810
was content simply committing robberies and
stabbing his supposed allies in the back.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:31.330
So, he eventually decided to open the Iceberg
Lounge as a means of proving he'd become a

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:32.950
legitimate businessman.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.880
Of course, it was a front all along.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.210
Batman, of course, sees right through it and
keeps tabs on what's going on behind the scenes

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.210
at the lounge.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.140
Over time, he has used it and its patrons
as a resource to study up on the happenings

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.290
of Gotham's criminal underworld.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:50.440
Other members of the Bat-family have visited
the Lounge for vastly different reasons.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.630
On one occasion, in the "Batman R.I.P."

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.900
storyline, both Robin and Batgirl went there
to forcefully gather what intel they could

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.850
from the Penguin on the missing Batman's whereabouts.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.540
And former Robin Jason Todd took over the
Iceberg Lounge for himself in the "Red Hood:

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.849
Outlaw" series, ousting Penguin from power
for a time and holding him captive.

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:13.410
Despite its prevalence on the page, a proper
live-action theatrical Iceberg Lounge has

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.170
only recently come to fruition.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.330
Director Christopher Nolan's "Dark Knight"
trilogy and the DC Extended Universe lacked

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.050
both it and the Penguin.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.610
"The Batman" marks its first live-action outing
at the movies.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:29.620
Colin Farrell's scarred, calculating take
on Oswald Cobblepot serves as its proprietor

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.690
and uses it as a cover for his criminal activities.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:36.720
Prior to "The Batman," The Iceberg Lounge
did appear in one live-action production:

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:37.720
"Gotham."

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.129
The Season 3 finale teased it, and it inevitably
became a focal point of Season 4.

00:02:42.129 --> 00:02:45.560
The Gotham nightclub has appeared in lots
of animated projects, however.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.870
It's been featured on the shows "The New Batman
Adventures" and "Justice League Unlimited,"

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:55.319
as well as the animated movies "Batman: Mystery
of the Batwoman" and "Batman: Assault on Arkham."

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:57.610
"Sorry, we're full up tonight.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.420
Shoo, shoo!"

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.650
It's perhaps been featured most prominently
in video games, though.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:07.510
The "Batman: Arkham" franchise endures as
one of the most widely acclaimed Batman adaptations,

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:13.120
tossing elements from a variety of Bat-projects
into a giant, grimy, gothic melting pot.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.480
Multiple games in the series also feature
the Iceberg Lounge in varying capacities,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.410
allowing players to explore it in all of its
glory as Batman himself.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:26.190
The Iceberg Lounge featured first in "Batman:
Arkham City" within the Cyrus Pinkney Institute

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:27.470
for Natural History.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:31.880
After Batman battles the Penguin , his numerous
cronies, and his horrifying "pets," Tiny the

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.400
shark and Solomon Grundy, you're free to look
around, solve riddles, and collect the building's

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.050
hidden Riddler Trophies.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.130
In both "Arkham City" and its sequel, "Batman:
Arkham Knight," the Iceberg Lounge also serves

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.030
as a combat challenge map.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:47.490
But you couldn't possibly chill at the Iceberg
Lounge in real life, right?

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.129
It's an entirely fictional place.

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:50.720
Well, maybe not.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.849
There is an Iceberg Lounge fans can visit
in Central London.

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:57.430
The venue promises "champagne, cocktails,
exceptional food, live entertainment, and

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:04.080
decadence" at a vaguely Batman-inspired corner
of the city appropriately called Park Row.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.810
Fans can experience their own little slice
of Gotham at a handful of other stops without

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.120
fear of being mugged by Joe Chill.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.810
Park Row includes attractions such as Pennyworth's
restaurant and bar, an art gallery called

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:19.340
Rogues Gallery, the Old Gotham City bar, and
the dinner-and-a-show spot Monarch Theater

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:24.089
— all paying subtle homage to the Caped
Crusader and his bevy of villains.

